Set the ICU default locale from --lang on every platform, not only ChromeOS.

Upstream sets it inside a `#if BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)` block, with a comment
explaining that the ChromeOS zygote starts before the browser and so needs it done
here. On Windows, macOS and Linux it is never set at all, and ICU keeps the system
locale for the life of the renderer.

That produces a realm that contradicts itself. A page reaches its locale through
Blink's per-context language and answers correctly, but anything that asks ICU
directly answers with the host's. Measured on a mac whose system locale is Russian,
on a profile claiming pl-PL:

    surface                page                    service worker
    navigator.language     pl-PL                   pl-PL
    Intl timeZone          Europe/Warsaw           Europe/Warsaw
    Intl resolved locale   pl-PL                   ru
    currency name          1,00 dolara ameryka...  1,00 доллара США
    month name             styczeń                 январь

creepjs reports exactly this pairing under ServiceWorkerGlobalScope and marks it
"confidence: high" — one scope answering half in the profile's language and half in
the host's is a stronger signal than either value alone.

Dedicated workers, shared workers, iframes and iframe-workers all probe clean; the
service worker is the only realm that reaches ICU without Blink in front of it.

--- a/content/renderer/renderer_main.cc
+++ b/content/renderer/renderer_main.cc
@@ -179,4 +179,30 @@ int RendererMain(MainFunctionParams parameters) {
   chromeos::system::EnableCoreSchedulingIfAvailable();
 #endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)

+#include "components/ungoogled/ungoogled_switches.h"
+
+#if !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+  // Everywhere else, ICU keeps the SYSTEM locale unless told otherwise, and a
+  // service worker asks ICU directly — Blink's per-context language never reaches
+  // it. So a profile could present pl-PL on the page and format currency in the
+  // host's language inside its own worker.
+  //
+  // The assignment is the same one ChromeOS already makes a few lines above; only
+  // its platform fence differs. Doing it for all platforms costs nothing on a
+  // machine whose --lang matches its system locale, which is the ordinary case.
+  //
+  // NOT --lang. Measured: the browser resolves --lang against the locales it ships
+  // and hands renderers the RESOLVED value, so --lang=pl-PL, --lang=de-DE and
+  // --lang=ja-JP all arrive as --lang=en-US in a build with only English resources.
+  // Reading it here produced an assignment that was faithful and useless.
+  // --fingerprint-language is forwarded verbatim, the way --timezone already is.
+  if (command_line.HasSwitch(switches::kFingerprintLanguage)) {
+    const std::string locale =
+        command_line.GetSwitchValueASCII(switches::kFingerprintLanguage);
+    if (!locale.empty()) {
+      base::i18n::SetICUDefaultLocale(locale);
+    }
+  }
+#endif  // !BUILDFLAG(IS_CHROMEOS)
+
   InitializeSkia();


